<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capitoline Hill</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Campidoglio, Capitol</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Capitoline Hill is one of the seven hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, overlooking the Forum. It held the city's chief temple, that of Jupiter Optimus Maximus, traditionally begun under the Tarquin kings and dedicated in 509 BCE, and served as Rome's religious and symbolic centre, where generals ended their triumphs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> later redesigned its piazza, the Campidoglio.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill.jpg" alt="Capitoline Hill" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pierre Dalbéra from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maquette_de_Rome_(mus%C3%A9e_de_la_civilisation_romaine,_Rome)_(5911810278).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
